The Security Council,

Recalling its relevant resolutions, including resolutions 242 (1967), 338 (1973), 446 (1979), 452 (1979), 465 (1980), 476 (1980), 478 (1980), 1397 (2002), 1515 (2003), 1850 (2008) and 2334 (2016),

Reaffirming the obligation of Israel, the occupying Power, to abide scrupulously by its legal obligations and responsibilities under the Fourth Geneva Convention relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, of 12 August 1949,

Reaffirming the inadmissibility of the acquisition of territory by force,

Recalling the obligation under the Quartet road map, endorsed by its resolution 1515 (2003), for a freeze by Israel of all settlement activity, including natural growth, and the dismantlement of all settlement outposts erected since March 2001,

Recalling also the obligation under the Quartet road map for the Palestinian Authority Security Forces to maintain effective operations aimed at confronting all those engaged in terror and dismantling terrorist capabilities, including the confiscation of illegal weapons,

Condemning all acts of violence against civilians, including acts of terror, as well as all acts of provocation, incitement and destruction,

Condemning the killing of Palestinian civilians by Israeli forces in the Gaza Strip, including children, and the firing of rockets from the Gaza Strip into Israel,

Stressing the importance of accountability and upholding international law and international humanitarian law,

Expressing grave concern at the deteriorating humanitarian situation in the Gaza Strip and recalling the obligation to ensure unimpeded access for humanitarian assistance,

Reiterating its vision of a region where two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, live side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders,

Stressing that the status quo is not sustainable and that significant steps are urgently needed to stabilize the situation and reverse negative trends on the ground, and to create conditions for successful final status negotiations,

1. Demands that Israel cease its use of force against Palestinian civilians, including in the Gaza Strip, and calls for the protection of Palestinian civilians;

2. Calls for immediate steps to prevent all acts of violence against civilians, including acts of terror, as well as all acts of provocation and destruction, calls for accountability in this regard, and calls for compliance with obligations under international law for the strengthening of ongoing efforts to combat terrorism, including through existing security coordination, and to clearly condemn all acts of terrorism;

3. Calls upon both parties to act on the basis of international law, including international humanitarian law, and their previous agreements and obligations, to observe calm and restraint, and to refrain from provocative actions, incitement and inflammatory rhetoric, with the aim, inter alia, of de-escalating the situation on the ground, rebuilding trust and confidence, demonstrating through policies and actions a genuine commitment to the two-State solution, and creating the conditions necessary for promoting peace;

4. Reaffirms the right to peaceful assembly and protest;

5. Calls upon all parties to continue, in the interest of the promotion of peace and security, to exert collective efforts to launch credible negotiations on all final status issues in the Middle East peace process and within the time frame specified by the Quartet in its statement of 21 September 2010;

6. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council every three months on the implementation of the provisions of the present resolution, including on the situation of children;

7. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

Recalling its relevant resolutions, including resolutions 242 (1967), 338 (1973), 446 (1979), 452 (1979), 465 (1980), 476 (1980), 478 (1980), 1397 (2002), 1515 (2003), 1850 (2008) and 2334 (2016),

Reaffirming the obligation of Israel, the occupying Power, to abide scrupulously by its legal obligations and responsibilities under the Fourth Geneva Convention relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, of 12 August 1949,

Reaffirming the inadmissibility of the acquisition of territory by force,

Recalling the obligation under the Quartet road map, endorsed by its resolution 1515 (2003), for a freeze by Israel of all settlement activity, including natural growth, and the dismantlement of all settlement outposts erected since March 2001,

Recalling also the obligation under the Quartet road map for the Palestinian Authority Security Forces to maintain effective operations aimed at confronting all those engaged in terror and dismantling terrorist capabilities, including the confiscation of illegal weapons,

Condemning all acts of violence against civilians, including acts of terror, as well as all acts of provocation, incitement and destruction,

Condemning all loss of civilian life, including the killing of Palestinian civilians by Israeli forces in the Gaza Strip, including children, and the killing of Israeli civilians by rocket and mortar fire from the Gaza Strip, and condemning in the strongest terms the rocket and mortar attacks by Hamas and other militant groups against Israeli civilians, as well as all acts of incitement and provocation,

Stressing the importance of accountability and upholding international law and international humanitarian law,

Expressing grave concern at the deteriorating humanitarian situation in the Gaza Strip and recalling the obligation to ensure unimpeded access for humanitarian assistance, including medical evacuations,

Reaffirming the right of all States to self-defence in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations and international law, and recognizing the legitimate security concerns of both Israelis and Palestinians,

Reiterating its vision of a region where two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, live side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders,

Stressing that the status quo is not sustainable and that significant steps are urgently needed to stabilize the situation and reverse negative trends on the ground, and to create conditions for successful final status negotiations,

1. Calls upon all parties to cease all acts of violence against civilians and to exercise maximum restraint; calls upon Israel to ensure that any use of force is necessary, proportionate and in full compliance with international humanitarian law and international human rights law, including the principles of distinction and proportionality, and to refrain from excessive force against Palestinian civilians, including in the Gaza Strip; and calls upon Hamas and other militant groups to immediately cease all rocket and mortar attacks and other acts of violence against Israeli civilians and to reject incitement to violence;

2. Calls for immediate steps to prevent all acts of violence against civilians, including acts of terror, as well as all acts of provocation and destruction, calls for accountability in this regard, and calls for compliance with obligations under international law for the strengthening of ongoing efforts to combat terrorism, including through existing security coordination, and to clearly condemn all acts of terrorism, including the firing of rockets and mortars from the Gaza Strip into Israel by Hamas and other militant groups, and to reject incitement to violence;

3. Calls for an independent, impartial, transparent and credible investigation into all deaths and injuries of civilians and all violations and abuses of international humanitarian law and human rights law in the Gaza Strip since 30 March 2018, including the killing of Palestinian civilians and attacks on Israeli civilians, and urges accountability for all violations;

4. Calls for immediate, safe and unimpeded humanitarian access to the Gaza Strip, including through the Kerem Shalom and Rafah crossings, and for medical evacuations and access for humanitarian and medical personnel, supplies and reconstruction materials, and urges the easing of restrictions that worsen the humanitarian situation;

5. Reaffirms the right to peaceful assembly and protest; stresses that protests must remain peaceful and must not be exploited by militant or terrorist groups to commit violence or attacks; and calls on all parties to ensure that security measures are necessary and proportionate and do not target peaceful protesters;

6. Calls upon both parties to act on the basis of international law, including international humanitarian law, and their previous agreements and obligations, to observe calm and restraint, and to refrain from provocative actions, incitement and inflammatory rhetoric, with the aim, inter alia, of de-escalating the situation on the ground, rebuilding trust and confidence, demonstrating through policies and actions a genuine commitment to the two-State solution, and creating the conditions necessary for promoting peace;

7. Calls for the immediate resumption of direct Israeli-Palestinian negotiations without preconditions, with the support of the Quartet and regional partners, aimed at achieving a comprehensive, just and lasting peace and a two-State solution;

8. Calls upon the Palestinian Authority and all Palestinian factions to comply with the Quartet requirements, including recognition of Israel, renunciation of violence and respect for previous agreements, and to take effective action against terrorism;

9. Reaffirms resolution 2334 (2016) and calls for its full implementation;

10. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council every three months on the implementation of the provisions of the present resolution, including on the situation of children, humanitarian access and medical evacuations, and violence and incitement by all parties;

11. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

